<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    RDR-194 Phase P3d (Decision D1, bead nexus-tk070.p3d): the composite FK
    `nexus.topic_assignments (tenant_id, source_collection, doc_id)` ->
    `nexus.chunks (tenant_id, collection, chash)`, and the one-for-one
    retirement of `ChashCensus` leg C1 (`dangling.topic_assignments`) plus
    its "either era" width tolerance — SAME commit as this file's VALIDATE,
    never earlier (D0.10; see `ChashCensus.java`'s own updated javadoc).

    THREE-STEP SHAPE (catalog-029 precedent, `catalog-029-manifest-chunk-
    fk.xml` — Hal disposition (a), T2 nexus/rdr-191-validate-placement-
    decision [22557], RDR-191 amendment (xi)):

      1. taxonomy-012-1: ADD CONSTRAINT ... NOT VALID.
      2. taxonomy-012-2: anti-join remediation — DELETE every
         topic_assignments row whose (tenant_id, source_collection,
         doc_id) has no matching nexus.chunks row (F17's shape: an
         anti-join against the chunk store, never a count comparison).
         Priced as safe: topic assignments are DERIVED data, recomputable
         by re-running assignment (RDR-194 D1's own remediation-ordering
         note; the writers' `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` makes recomputation
         idempotent).
      3. taxonomy-012-3: VALIDATE CONSTRAINT.

    All three are ORDINARY RUN-ONCE changesets (no runAlways), ship
    TOGETHER in ONE file, in ONE release — changeset 1 always runs before
    changeset 3 in the same migration walk, so if changeset 1 succeeds the
    constraint necessarily exists when changeset 3 runs. No SEPARATE-
    RELEASE gap window exists here (unlike fk-002-validate.xml's own
    history), but changeset 3 still carries a whole-changeset MARK_RAN
    precondition to satisfy the project's blanket "every bare VALIDATE
    CONSTRAINT is guarded" tripwire (tests/test_changelog_validate_
    precondition_lint.py), which does not carve out a same-release
    exception — matching catalog-029-2's own reasoning verbatim.

    FK SHAPE (RDR-194 D1, `rdr-194-fk-census-structural-schema-warts.md`
    Decision D1 — read that section before touching this):
      - ON UPDATE CASCADE (mandatory, D1): nexus.chunks is the PARENT; a
        collection rename or an RDR-180-style chash rekey is a parent-key
        UPDATE that must propagate to the assignment, not fail. The
        SIBLING FK already on this exact table,
        `topic_assignments_collection_fk` (fk-002-5, `(tenant_id,
        source_collection)` -> `catalog_collections(tenant_id, name)`),
        already uses ON UPDATE CASCADE for the identical reason — house
        pattern, not a novel choice.
      - ON DELETE CASCADE (D1, deliberately NOT the catalog-029/fk-002
        DEFERRABLE-NO-ACTION shape): an assignment whose chunk is gone is
        exactly the dangling-row population `ChashCensus` leg C1 exists to
        report — CASCADE removes it structurally, which is what retires
        the leg. Unlike catalog-029's manifest FK, no class-B deferred-
        constraint site is needed anywhere for this FK: CASCADE lets
        PostgreSQL remove the referencing topic_assignments rows AS PART
        OF the same DELETE statement against nexus.chunks, with no
        caller-side `SET CONSTRAINTS ... DEFERRED` ordering dance required
        (that machinery exists only for a NO ACTION/RESTRICT delete side,
        which this FK does not use).
      - Natural key throughout: `(tenant_id, source_collection, doc_id)`,
        the same triple shape as `nexus.chunks`' own PK
        (`vectors-004-unify-chunks.xml`) — never a metadata copy.
      - MATCH SIMPLE (the PostgreSQL FK default): `source_collection` is
        NOT NULL (taxonomy-010-source-collection-backfill.xml's `SET NOT
        NULL`, P3b) and `doc_id` is NOT NULL (taxonomy-001-baseline.xml)
        — MATCH SIMPLE's null-exemption is structurally inert here,
        exactly as catalog-029's own header documents for the identical
        reason on its FK. This is also the P3d non-vacuity check named in
        the bead's acceptance criteria: a table left mostly NULL on
        `source_collection` would validate green while enforcing nothing;
        P3b's `SET NOT NULL` is the structural defence closing that gap
        BEFORE this changeset ever runs.

    RESOLVABLE-ONLY DISCIPLINE AT THE APPLICATION LAYER (RDR-194 P3d scope
    enlargement, deep-analyst cncue analysis, T2 nexus/cncue-finalize-
    reject-reconciliation [22750] §2b/§4): this FK, once VALIDATEd, makes
    it possible for `StagingPromoteOps.finalizeTenant`'s topic_assignments
    promote to violate it — a CONFORMANT 64-hex staged doc_id whose
    content collection has not promoted to `nexus.chunks` yet would
    otherwise hit SQLSTATE 23503 and abort the WHOLE tenant finalize
    transaction, naming no offending row. That promote path (companion
    commit, same bead) now anti-joins against `nexus.chunks` itself before
    inserting, so an unresolvable-yet-conformant row stays STAGED and
    counted rather than reaching this FK at all — the FK is the STORAGE
    guarantee; the anti-join is the APPLICATION-side deferral that keeps
    the storage guarantee from ever firing on the ordinary "topics ETL
    landed before the content collection did" case.

    RLS DISCIPLINE (taxonomy-012-2 only; changesets 1/3 are pure DDL/
    VALIDATE, no DML): nexus.topic_assignments and nexus.chunks both carry
    FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY (taxonomy-001-baseline.xml,
    vectors-004-unify-chunks.xml). Liquibase runs as nexus_admin, the
    table owner but NOT BYPASSRLS (role-001-nexus-svc.xml) — under FORCE
    RLS with no nexus.tenant GUC set at migration time, an untoggled read
    of nexus.chunks returns ZERO rows for every tenant, which would make
    the anti-join misclassify EVERY topic_assignments row as dangling and
    delete the whole table (the identical trap catalog-029-1's own header
    documents for its manifest anti-join against the same nexus.chunks
    table). Both tables are toggled NO FORCE, then restored to FORCE,
    symmetrically, inside taxonomy-012-2's own changeset (nexus-1wjmq
    toggle-wrapped shape).

    NO LOCK TABLE ... ACCESS EXCLUSIVE is needed before taxonomy-012-2's
    DELETE, for the identical reason catalog-029-1's header gives: changeset
    taxonomy-012-1 (in this SAME migration walk, always immediately before
    taxonomy-012-2) already adds the FK NOT VALID, which enforces EVERY new
    write from the instant it commits — no writer running after
    taxonomy-012-1 commits can create a NEW dangling row, so taxonomy-012-2's
    anti-join only ever needs to clean up rows that predate this walk.

    CENSUS LEG RETIREMENT (D0.10, same commit as this file's VALIDATE, in
    the COMPANION Java commit, never split across two commits): `ChashCensus`
    leg C1 (`dangling.topic_assignments`, `unresolvableBytesCount` against
    `EITHER_ERA_SHAPE`) retires along with the "either era" width tolerance
    it exists to apply — the FK this file adds makes the population it
    scans for structurally impossible from this migration forward. Legs C2
    (`frecency.chunk_id`) and C3 (`relevance_log.chunk_id`) are UNCHANGED —
    they stay on `CANONICAL_SHAPE` (nexus-lgdel.l1); C4 is untouched. See
    `ChashCensus.java`'s own updated javadoc for the mechanical diff.

    CONSEQUENCE RECORDED (so no later reader mistakes the cascade for data
    loss): with ON DELETE CASCADE, any deletion of a `nexus.chunks` row
    (`purge_trash`'s orphan-chunk sweep, RDR-192-class superseded-chunk
    reaping, collection deletion) now also removes that chunk's
    `topic_assignments` rows. That IS the intended semantics — an
    assignment for a chunk that no longer exists is exactly the dangling
    leg `ChashCensus` C1 used to report — and it is precisely what makes
    C1's retirement above correct rather than a detection regression.

    Placed after `taxonomy-011-doc-id-bytea.xml` (current tail) and before
    the `grants-nexus-svc.xml` runAlways block, matching the established
    late-append convention (D0.1; see catalog-031's / telemetry-004's /
    aspects-003's / plans-002's / taxonomy-008's / catalog-032's /
    remap-003's / taxonomy-009's / legacy-001's / legacy-002's /
    taxonomy-010's / taxonomy-011's own `<include>` comments for the
    identical reasoning). `EXPECT_NEW_CHANGESETS=3`, re-pinned to this
    file as authored.
-->
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.4.xsd">

    <!-- ── Changeset taxonomy-012-1: ADD CONSTRAINT ... NOT VALID ───────────── -->
    <changeSet id="taxonomy-012-1" author="nexus-tk070.p3d">
        <comment>
            topic_assignments_chunk_fk: FOREIGN KEY (tenant_id, source_collection,
            doc_id) REFERENCES nexus.chunks (tenant_id, collection, chash) ON UPDATE
            CASCADE ON DELETE CASCADE NOT VALID. ON UPDATE CASCADE mirrors the
            sibling topic_assignments_collection_fk (fk-002-5) already on this
            table. ON DELETE CASCADE (RDR-194 D1) rather than catalog-029's
            DEFERRABLE NO ACTION shape: an assignment whose chunk is gone is
            exactly the dangling row ChashCensus leg C1 exists to report, and
            CASCADE removes it structurally with no caller-side deferred-
            constraint ordering needed. NOT VALID: enforces every new write
            immediately; retroactive validation of pre-existing rows is
            taxonomy-012-2 (remediate) then taxonomy-012-3 (VALIDATE).
        </comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true" stripComments="false">
ALTER TABLE nexus.topic_assignments
    ADD CONSTRAINT topic_assignments_chunk_fk
    FOREIGN KEY (tenant_id, source_collection, doc_id)
    REFERENCES nexus.chunks (tenant_id, collection, chash)
    ON UPDATE CASCADE
    ON DELETE CASCADE
    NOT VALID;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            ALTER TABLE nexus.topic_assignments DROP CONSTRAINT IF EXISTS topic_assignments_chunk_fk;
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <!-- ── Changeset taxonomy-012-2: anti-join remediation (F17 shape) ──────── -->
    <changeSet id="taxonomy-012-2" author="nexus-tk070.p3d">
        <comment>
            Delete every topic_assignments row whose (tenant_id, source_collection,
            doc_id) has no matching nexus.chunks row -- an anti-join against the
            chunk store (F17), never a count comparison. RLS toggle-wrapped
            (nexus-1wjmq shape): both nexus.topic_assignments and nexus.chunks
            carry FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY and Liquibase's nexus_admin owner is
            not BYPASSRLS, so both are toggled off for the DELETE and restored
            before this changeset ends. The deleted-row count is surfaced via
            RAISE NOTICE, captured by Liquibase's JdbcExecutor from the JDBC
            Statement's SQLWarning chain (SHOW_SQL_WARNING_MESSAGES defaults
            true) -- no runAlways, no DDL-in-Python, no bespoke Java capture.
            Priced as safe: topic assignments are DERIVED data, recomputable by
            re-running assignment (RDR-194 D1), and the writers' own
            ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING makes recomputation idempotent.
        </comment>
        <sql splitStatements="false" stripComments="false">
ALTER TABLE nexus.topic_assignments NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE nexus.chunks NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

DO $$
DECLARE
    v_deleted_count BIGINT;
BEGIN
    DELETE FROM nexus.topic_assignments ta
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (
        SELECT 1 FROM nexus.chunks c
         WHERE c.tenant_id  = ta.tenant_id
           AND c.collection = ta.source_collection
           AND c.chash      = ta.doc_id
    );
    GET DIAGNOSTICS v_deleted_count = ROW_COUNT;
    RAISE NOTICE 'RDR-194 P3d (nexus-tk070.p3d): deleted % dangling topic_assignments row(s) with no matching nexus.chunks row', v_deleted_count;
END $$;

ALTER TABLE nexus.chunks FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE nexus.topic_assignments FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <!-- No rollback: the deleted rows are unrecoverable, dangling,
                 derived bookkeeping (RDR-194 D1 prices this deletion as safe;
                 topic assignments are recomputable by re-running assignment).
                 Rolling back the FK/VALIDATE is taxonomy-012-1's / taxonomy-012-3's
                 own rollback. -->
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <!-- ── Changeset taxonomy-012-3: VALIDATE CONSTRAINT ─────────────────────── -->
    <changeSet id="taxonomy-012-3" author="nexus-tk070.p3d">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <sqlCheck expectedResult="1">SELECT COUNT(*) FROM pg_constraint WHERE conname = 'topic_assignments_chunk_fk'</sqlCheck>
        </preConditions>
        <comment>
            VALIDATE topic_assignments_chunk_fk -- retroactively check pre-existing
            rows now that taxonomy-012-2 has remediated any dangling population.
            Guarded by a whole-changeset precondition (nexus-4m6i0.13/nexus-ms57z
            shape 1, same as fk-002-7..11/fk-003-7..11/catalog-029-2): skips (marks
            ran, once) instead of hard-ERRORing a crash-loop boot on any box where
            the constraint doesn't exist. All three taxonomy-012 changesets ship
            together in one file/release, so this guard is expected to be a
            permanent no-op in practice (taxonomy-012-1 always runs first in the
            same walk) -- it is added purely to satisfy the project's blanket
            "every bare VALIDATE CONSTRAINT is guarded" tripwire (tests/
            test_changelog_validate_precondition_lint.py), which does not carve
            out a same-release exception.
        </comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true" stripComments="false">
ALTER TABLE nexus.topic_assignments VALIDATE CONSTRAINT topic_assignments_chunk_fk;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <!-- VALIDATE cannot be un-done (no INVALIDATE in PG); the constraint
                 itself is dropped by taxonomy-012-1's rollback. -->
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
